WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:04.260
Even though Kevin Smith
is a huge pop culture fan

00:00:04.266 --> 00:00:07.900
and science fiction
fan, he remains

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:11.233
science-curiosity challenged.

00:00:11.233 --> 00:00:12.100
No.

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:13.233
Yeah, Yeah.

00:00:13.233 --> 00:00:14.433
I have evidence of that.

00:00:14.433 --> 00:00:16.000
Let's check it out.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:19.467
You're talking to
a man, who at age 46,

00:00:19.467 --> 00:00:22.934
is still not quite sure
how the water works

00:00:22.934 --> 00:00:25.967
when I turn on the spigot.

00:00:25.967 --> 00:00:27.734
So mysteries of the universe.

00:00:27.734 --> 00:00:29.133
I got one for you, ready?

00:00:29.133 --> 00:00:30.800
The refrigerator light.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:32.000
That one I get.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:33.567
There will be a button
that pushes off.

00:00:33.567 --> 00:00:34.567
Oh, you found the button.

00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:36.800
That's where my
science begins and ends.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:38.467
It takes some curiosity.

00:00:38.467 --> 00:00:41.033
The faucet, like it's
not plugged into anything.

00:00:41.033 --> 00:00:44.300
And like so, wait, this pressure
is just waiting at all times.

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:46.900
Like, isn't this a ticking
time bomb waiting to go off?

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:48.400
I realize I'm not smart enough.

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:51.133
I'm smart about one
thing, things that don't

00:00:51.133 --> 00:00:53.400
exist, comic books, movies, TV.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:55.000
I wasn't even smart about
it, I just liked that.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:56.266
That's where I
invested all my time.

00:00:56.266 --> 00:00:58.800
Not even in sports,
another unreal thing.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:02.467
I invested my life, early
life, in cartoons and comics

00:01:02.467 --> 00:01:05.100
and movies and TV and
fun things and sci-fi.

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:07.333
And so it just worked out.

00:01:07.333 --> 00:01:09.900
I don't know what I'd be doing
right now if the world didn't

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:12.433
fall the way it did,
but it just worked out

00:01:12.433 --> 00:01:15.567
that right now, people are
interested in those things,

00:01:15.567 --> 00:01:16.734
culturally.

00:01:16.734 --> 00:01:19.467
It's no longer pop
culture, it is the culture.

00:01:19.467 --> 00:01:22.500
And because I was raised in
it, I'm well-versed in it,

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:24.900
suddenly there's this
weird the universe

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:27.700
aligns, as you know, in
some times and some moments

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:29.100
and it has given
me a path that's

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:30.567
like, so I could
literally just be

00:01:30.567 --> 00:01:32.834
a fan of things for a living?

00:01:32.834 --> 00:01:34.467
This is a job?

00:01:34.467 --> 00:01:36.767
And so that's kind
of what I've--

00:01:36.767 --> 00:01:38.300
you know, I love
making films and stuff.

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:40.400
But really, that's only
one half of my career.

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:43.467
The rest of it is just spent
mostly celebrating things

00:01:43.467 --> 00:01:46.633
that I enjoy, largely, of
course, a lot of it centered

00:01:46.633 --> 00:01:48.600
around the unreal sci-fi.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:52.066
That's where my sci
ends, with a fi.

00:01:52.066 --> 00:01:55.333
You've made a brilliant
career out of the sci.

00:01:55.333 --> 00:01:59.166
Well I have used
people such as yourself,

00:01:59.166 --> 00:02:03.233
communities that you
represent, and explored ways

00:02:03.233 --> 00:02:05.033
to attach real science to it.

00:02:05.033 --> 00:02:09.500
And I have found that if you
identify a fan base that has

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:13.433
rabid interest in a thing
and I find a way to attach

00:02:13.433 --> 00:02:17.400
something real to it, oh,
my God, give me all of that

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:18.600
that you can.

